{pad:meta title='Conditions and Branching'/}

<p>The <code>{pad:open/}pad:if{pad:close/}</code> pair renders its content when its expression holds, with
as many <code>{pad:open/}pad:elseif{pad:close/}</code> branches as needed and an
<code>{pad:open/}pad:else{pad:close/}</code> for when none did:</p>

{common:example 'fragments/conditions_1'/}

<h2>The comparison operators</h2>

<table border="1" cellpadding="8" cellspacing="0" style="border-collapse: collapse;">
  <tr>
    <th>operator</th>
    <th>meaning</th>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><code>eq</code> or <code>==</code></td>
    <td>equal</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><code>ne</code> or <code>!=</code></td>
    <td>not equal</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><code>gt</code> / <code>lt</code></td>
    <td>greater / less than</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><code>ge</code> / <code>le</code></td>
    <td>greater / less or equal</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><code>and</code> / <code>or</code></td>
    <td>both hold / either holds</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><code>range (a, b)</code></td>
    <td>the value lies between a and b, ends included</td>
  </tr>
</table>

<p>&nbsp;</p>

{common:example 'fragments/conditions_2'/}

<p><b>A bare value is tested for its truth.</b> <code>{pad:open/}pad:if $flag{pad:close/}</code> holds
unless the value is empty, <code>0</code>, <code>'0'</code> or FALSE - the truth PHP gives it;
write a comparison when a particular value is meant: <code>{pad:open/}pad:if $count eq 0{pad:close/}</code>.</p>

<h2>Case and when</h2>

<p>When one value decides between several branches, <code>{pad:open/}pad:case{pad:close/}</code> reads
better than a chain of elseifs. Each <code>{pad:open/}pad:when{pad:close/}</code> names a value, and
<code>{pad:open/}pad:else{pad:close/}</code> catches the rest:</p>

{common:example 'fragments/conditions_3'/}

<p>A <code>{pad:open/}pad:when{pad:close/}</code> may list several values, separated by commas; it answers
when any of them matches:</p>

{common:example 'fragments/conditions_7'/}

<h2>The ternary</h2>

<p>Inside an expression tag, <code>?</code> and <code>:</code> pick one of two values - the
usual way to give alternating rows their class, together with the
<a href="?pages/properties">iteration properties</a>:</p>

{common:example 'fragments/conditions_4'/}

<h2>Inside an expression</h2>

<p>An expression has a ternary of its own, <code>cond ? then : else</code>, the weakest
binding there is. <code>a ?? b</code> answers <code>a</code> unless it is empty or a field
that is not there - so it gives a field its default - and the tests <code>in</code>,
<code>like</code>, <code>matches</code> and <code>range</code> read infix:</p>

{common:example 'fragments/conditions_6'/}

<h2>Properties in conditions</h2>

<p>The <a href="?pages/properties">iteration properties</a> read as values inside an
expression, so they stand in conditions - alone for the boolean ones, with an operator for
the counting ones:</p>

{common:example 'fragments/conditions_5'/}

<p>Every tag also carries the <a href="?pages/else">else construct</a>: the part after the
divider renders when the tag itself produced nothing.</p>
